        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "text": "## Finding: an event was created 75 minutes after Pending Close\n\nFor Modine’s Racine operation, manufacturing order **MO-25-09-1070** corresponds to work order **WO-2025-09-004** for a **Cooler Plate Pack**.\n\n| Audit item | Recorded value |\n|---|---|\n| Source transaction | **TXN-ISSUE-250930-1070** |\n| Cost event | **CE-250904-0004** |\n| Event type | **INPUT — component issue** |\n| Amount | **USD $284,763.42** — 28,476,342 cents |\n| Cost period | **Sep-25**, `PER-COST-2025-09` |\n| Exact event-created time | **2025-09-30T19:15:00** |\n| Exact Pending Close time | **2025-09-30T18:00:00** |\n| Time after Pending Close | **75 minutes — 1 hour 15 minutes** |\n| Recorded accounting status | **POSTED** |\n\n**Calculation:** `19:15:00 − 18:00:00 = 4,500 seconds ÷ 60 = 75 minutes`.\n\nThe timestamps above are reproduced exactly; the source does not specify a time zone.\n\n### Policy conclusion\n\n**The recorded POSTED status is not consistent with Oracle’s documented Pending Close treatment for this event.**\n\nOracle Fusion Cloud SCM Cost Management 26B states that transactions created after the Pending Close date, even when relating to the current or an earlier period, **“won’t be processed.”** New transactions are held from staging/processing until the period is closed or reopened. Revenue lines, COGS recognition, and manual cost adjustments are exceptions; this component-issue **INPUT** event is not one of them.\n\n**Sources:** `work_orders`, `cost_periods`, and `work_order_cost_events` in `system/oracle_scm_cost_management/state.db`; Oracle 26B documentation, **“Cost Accounting Period Statuses and Transaction Accounting,” printed page 225 / PDF page 233**.\n\nThe review was read-only; **no database records were changed**. The audit result and provenance were saved to `/cache/work/worker_85c0bb4450b2/audit-report.json`.",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": []
        }
      ],
      "phase": "final_answer"
    }
